00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,897
Confound regressors.

2
00:00:00,897 --> 00:00:05,833
Model nuisance variation without treating it as the effect of interest.

3
00:00:05,833 --> 00:00:12,115
The goal is clear: Explain why and how nuisance regressors enter a first-level design.

4
00:00:12,965 --> 00:00:14,041
Confound regressors.

5
00:00:14,041 --> 00:00:21,579
Confound regressors capture structured variation—such as motion—that is not the experimental effect of interest.

6
00:00:21,579 --> 00:00:29,117
They reduce bias when specified appropriately, but they do not automatically repair poor data.

7
00:00:29,117 --> 00:00:38,271
Motion estimates, non-steady-state volumes, physiological terms, or fMRIPrep confounds may be used according to a documented strategy.

8
00:00:38,271 --> 00:00:42,578
Check column count, missing values, and time points.

9
00:00:42,578 --> 00:00:47,962
A confound file must have one row per modeled volume.

10
00:00:48,812 --> 00:00:52,044
Now connect the idea to an observable check.

11
00:00:52,044 --> 00:00:54,872
First, find the number of bold volumes.

12
00:00:54,872 --> 00:00:58,507
Next, count rows and columns in the confound file.

13
00:00:58,507 --> 00:01:03,355
Then, confirm there are no text headers if FEAT expects numeric input.

14
00:01:03,355 --> 00:01:06,586
After that, document why each column is included.

15
00:01:06,586 --> 00:01:09,818
The command shown on screen is an example.

16
00:01:09,818 --> 00:01:15,877
Adapt its path to your own working directory and verify the target before running it.

17
00:01:16,727 --> 00:01:19,004
Quality control comes before interpretation.

18
00:01:19,004 --> 00:01:24,922
The bold series and confound file have the same number of time points.

19
00:01:24,922 --> 00:01:32,207
A useful self-check is this: In one or two sentences, what decision does “Confound regressors” support?

20
00:01:32,207 --> 00:01:36,760
Explain why and how nuisance regressors enter a first-level design.

21
00:01:37,610 --> 00:01:44,026
To recap: First, model nuisance variation without treating it as the effect of interest.

22
